---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Rail from '../../components/Rail.astro';
import { SERIES } from '../../data/series';
import { allNotes, inSeries, noteUrl, readingTime } from '../../lib/notes';

const series = SERIES['inference-engineering'];
const chapters = inSeries(await allNotes(), 'inference-engineering');
const minutes = chapters.reduce((sum, note) => sum + readingTime(note.body), 0);
---

<Base title={series.title} description={series.blurb}>
  <Rail slot="rail" />
  <section class="hello col">
    <h1>{series.title}</h1>
    <p class="lede">{series.blurb}</p>
  </section>

  <section class="home-section prose">
    <p>
      Calling a model through an API feels simple. You send text, text comes back. Somewhere behind that call,
      someone had to work out which GPU to use, how to fit forty people onto it at once, what to do when four
      hundred turn up, and how to keep the bill from eating the product. That work has a name now: inference
      engineering.
    </p>
    <p>
      I wanted to understand it properly, so I am reading
      <a href={series.book!.url} rel="noopener"><cite>{series.book!.title}</cite></a> by {series.book!.author} and
      writing each part down in my own words. Every chapter here starts from a question, builds the idea with a
      small example, and only then gets into how it is done. Where I could, I measured things on my own laptop
      instead of taking them on trust. Each chapter ends by pointing at the pages of the book that go deeper.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you have not met tokens, attention or the generation loop before, read
      <a href="/notes/how-llms-work">How an LLM works, end to end</a> first. Everything here builds on it. To try
      the same ideas with your hands, there is <a href="/notes/basics-playground">a page of toys</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="home-section" aria-labelledby="chapters-h">
    <h2 class="side" id="chapters-h">{chapters.length} chapters, about {Math.round(minutes / 5) * 5} minutes in all</h2>
    <ol class="chapters">
      {
        chapters.map((note) => (
          <li>
            <span class="num">{note.data.chapter}</span>
            <div>
              <a href={noteUrl(note.id)}>{note.data.title}</a>
              <p>{note.data.description}</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .chapters {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.25rem;
  }

  .chapters li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 2rem 1fr;
    gap: 0 0.5rem;
  }

  .num {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.9;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  .chapters a {
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .chapters p {
    margin: 0.25rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
</style>
